\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A minimum reliable optimization answer.
\series default
 Write the choices, fixed parameters, domains and actual constraints. Compute the derivatives and state the full KKT conditions with a consistent sign convention. Solve one candidate; test its feasibility and any zero-choice entry condition. Recover the original choices, check the budget, and explain why the KKT certificate is globally sufficient. A negative interior candidate changes the active set, not the given parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
A minimum reliable revealed-preference answer.
\series default
 Identify which regime supplies the bundle and which regime supplies the comparison prices and income. Write the event implication first, then the probability inequality. Carry numerical counts through to shares, the correctly directed welfare interval, and a one-sentence conclusion. Independent cross-sections do not provide household pairings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Start small.
\series default
 Reproduce one complete household regime and the two-regime numerical welfare calculation without the key. Then attempt an altered regime and one short maximum-comparison or event-inclusion proof. These are practice milestones, not a prediction of a core-exam result or a substitute for the full syllabus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Reading map.
\series default
 Question 1 covers expenditure coordinates, KKT, human-search corners, agent pricing and finite-data maximum comparisons. Question 2 covers cross-affordability, welfare bounds, overlap and transitivity. The appendix offers optional shorter optimization routes; it is not an additional exam requirement.
