 The agent operates in the 
\begin_inset Formula $A=8$
\end_inset

 environment. Let 
\begin_inset Formula $W(b)$
\end_inset

 be optimized household utility before the fee. The household's outside option is the powerful tool, 
\begin_inset Formula $W(0)=81/64$
\end_inset

, not manual search. Its willingness to pay for delegation is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(b)=W(b)-W(0)$
\end_inset

. Because the numeraire stays positive, a fixed fee reduces optimized utility one-for-one and does not change 
\begin_inset Formula $a,e,c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Revenue is increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

, so the sharing restriction binds: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
F(b)=\frac{1}{4}\Delta(b),\qquad\Pi(b)=\frac{1}{4}[W(b)-W(0)]-\frac{b^{2}}{7680}.
\]
\end_inset

The supplied envelope identity gives 
\begin_inset Formula $W'(b)=1/(D^{2}e)$
\end_inset

. With 
\begin_inset Formula $A=8$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r>0$
\end_inset

, human search is positive when 
\begin_inset Formula $b<15$
\end_inset

, then reaches zero at 
\begin_inset Formula $b=15$
\end_inset

. Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \[
W(b)=\begin{cases}
81/64+b/64, & 0\le b\le15,\\
2-2/\sqrt{1+b}, & b\ge15,
\end{cases}\quad W'(b)=\begin{cases}
1/64, & 0\le b\le15,\\
(1+b)^{-3/2}, & b\ge15.
\end{cases}
\]
\end_inset

There is no derivative discontinuity at 
\begin_inset Formula $b=15$
\end_inset

. For 
\begin_inset Formula $b\le15$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi'(b)=1/256-b/3840$
\end_inset

, which vanishes at 
\begin_inset Formula $b=15$
\end_inset

. Beyond 15, the marginal benefit decreases and the marginal cost increases, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi'(b)<0$
\end_inset

. Alternatively, the given concavity of 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 and strictly convex cost imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 is strictly concave: its stationary point is the unique global maximum. Thus 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\boxed{b^{*}=15,\quad F^{*}=\frac{15}{256},\quad C(b^{*})=\frac{15}{512},\quad\Pi^{*}=\frac{15}{512}>0.}
\]
\end_inset

No service 
\begin_inset Formula $(b,F)=(0,0)$
\end_inset

 yields zero profit and is dominated. At the optimal contract, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
(a,e,c,p,r)=\left(0,\frac{1}{4},4,\frac{1}{16},\frac{433}{256}\right),\qquad V=W(15)-F^{*}=\frac{369}{256}.
\]
\end_inset

The tool's utility was 
\begin_inset Formula $81/64=324/256$
\end_inset

, so the consumer gains 
\begin_inset Formula $45/256>0$
\end_inset

. Yet outside consumption falls from 
\begin_inset Formula $7/4=448/256$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $433/256$
\end_inset

: the fee exceeds the change in grocery expenditure, which is zero here. The welfare gain instead includes eliminating human effort 
\begin_inset Formula $15/8$
\end_inset

. A strict utility gain does not require increased cash.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Weak voluntary participation only imposes 
\begin_inset Formula $F\le\Delta(b)$
\end_inset

. A monopolist could set equality and leave zero consumer surplus. Here the explicit quarter-share cap guarantees a strictly positive consumer share whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(b)>0$
\end_inset

.
