 This subpart uses a separate interior panel and the known price technology, as stated in the revised exam. At observation 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, utility is strictly increasing in outside consumption, so the budget is exhausted: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
r_{t}=M_{t}-p_{t}c_{t}.
\]
\end_inset

Here 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{t}$
\end_inset

 is income net of the date-specific fixed fee. For a comparison pair with 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{jt}c_{j}\le M_{t}$
\end_inset

, define the counterfactual outside consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{jt}=M_{t}-q_{jt}c_{j}\ge0$
\end_inset

. Then 
\begin_inset Formula $(c_{j},a_{j},r_{jt})$
\end_inset

 is feasible at date t, with exactly the same AI assistance as the observed choice. By the definition of a maximum, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
U_{t}+r_{t}\ge U_{j}+r_{jt}.
\]
\end_inset

Substitute the two outside-consumption expressions and cancel income: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
U_{t}+M_{t}-p_{t}c_{t} & \ge U_{j}+M_{t}-q_{jt}c_{j},\\
U_{j}-U_{t} & \le q_{jt}c_{j}-p_{t}c_{t}.
\end{align*}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $(c_{j},a_{j})\ne(c_{t},a_{t})$
\end_inset

, the counterfactual is different from the unique maximizer. The maximum comparison, and hence the final inequality, is strict. If the consumption-search pair is identical, both prices are evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $(A_{t},b_{t})$
\end_inset

 and the inequality is equality, even when incomes or observed outside consumption differ across dates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The correct counterfactual price is 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{jt}=p(a_{j};A_{t},b_{t})$
\end_inset

: it combines the alternative's human search with date t's tool and contracted service. Using 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j}=p(a_{j};A_{j},b_{j})$
\end_inset

 would compare a different environment. If 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{jt}c_{j}>M_{t}$
\end_inset

, the alternative requires negative outside consumption and is not feasible; the displayed comparison is not imposed for that pair. No derivative, concavity, supporting plane, or multiplier is required in this subpart. These are necessary finite-data restrictions, not a sufficient rationalizability theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Optional original-coordinate route.
\series default
 One may write the same maximum comparison directly as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
u(c_{t},a_{t})+M_{t}-p(a_{t};A_{t},b_{t})c_{t}\ge u(c_{j},a_{j})+M_{t}-p(a_{j};A_{t},b_{t})c_{j}.
\]
\end_inset

This is the same proof in original coordinates; counterfactual feasibility and strictness must still be checked.
\end_layout

